      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go a bit deeper into the leucine aspect? Specifically, how does the sensing of intracellular leucine levels actually signal to the mTORC1 complex—is it happening at the lysosomal membrane or somewhere else? I want to make sure I give my client a really high-level explanation of the cellular mechanism.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood, I'll dive deeper. Intracellular leucine concentration is sensed at the lysosomal membrane-bound sensor, the Regulated in Development and Cell Death 1 (Receptor-Activated Serine/Threonine Protein Kinase 2, RAS p61) (RAB31) and its coactivator, Matchless 6 (MM6A1/2). Upon sufficient leucine levels, RAS p61 and MM6A1 localize to the lysosome and upstream of the lysosomal enzyme cathepsin D. The activation of RAS p61 induces an effector cascade that leads to the involvement of the Sestrin2 protein, which sequesters and dephosphorylates TSC2 to disengage TSC complex 1 (TSC1/2), eventually relieving the inhibition of mTORC1. This results in intracellular signaling that promotes protein synthesis, which is hugely important for muscle hypertrophy. By elaborating this pathway to your client, you'll offer not just the cellular specifics, but also the importance of protein quality—high levels of essential amino acids like leucine drive the body to engage in these complex signaling networks.",
      "source": "target",
